JavaScript Fetch API:

- 💡 The Fetch API interface allows web browser to make HTTP requests to web servers.
- 😀 No need for XMLHttpRequest anymore.
- fetch() is an ES6 feature.
- ES6 is fully supported in all modern browsers since June 2017:
- eg: The example below fetches a file and displays the content:

        <p id="demo">Fetch a file to change this text</p>

        <script>
            let file = "fetch_info.txt"
            
            fetch(file)
            .then(x => x.text())
            .then(y => document.getElementById("demo").innerHTML = y);
        </script>


    💡 Since Fetch is based on async and await, the example above might be easier to understand like this:

        <p id="demo2">Fetch a file to change this text</p>

        <script>
            function myDisplay(something) {
                document.getElementById("demo2").innerHTML = something;
            }

            getText("fetch_info.txt");

            async function getText(file) {
                let x = await fetch(file);
                let y = await x.text();
                myDisplay(y);
            }
        </script>
    

    💡 Or even better: Use understandable names instead of x and y:

        async function getText(file) {
            let myObject = await fetch(file);
            let myText = await myObject.text();
            myDisplay(myText);
        }